.TH POLL 2
.SH NAME
poll \- wait for one of several file descriptors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.ft B
#include <poll.h>

int poll(struct pollfd *\fIfds\fP, nfds_t \fInfds\fP, int \fItimeout\fP)
.fi
.ft R
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Poll
looks at the
.I nfds
entries of
.IR fds ,
each a descriptor
.RI ( fd ),
what to wait for
.RI ( events )
and what happened
.RI ( revents ,
set by the call).
It returns as soon as one of them is ready, or after
.I timeout
milliseconds (\-1: no timeout; 0: do not wait).
.PP
The events are
.B POLLIN
(data to read, also
.BR POLLRDNORM )
and
.B POLLOUT
(writing will not block, also
.BR POLLWRNORM ).
.B POLLERR
(an error; for a pipe: no reader left),
.B POLLHUP
(no writer left on a pipe or FIFO) and
.B POLLNVAL
(the descriptor is not open) are reported whether asked for or not.
An entry with a negative
.I fd
is ignored and gets
.I revents
0.
.PP
Pipes and FIFOs are checked by the file system.
A character device is asked by its driver (DEV_SELECT); a device whose driver
does not answer that counts as always ready, like regular files, directories
and block devices.
.SH "RETURN VALUE
The number of entries with a nonzero
.IR revents ,
0 on a timeout, \-1 on an error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP 15
[EINTR]
A signal came before anything was ready.
.TP 15
[EINVAL]
.I nfds
is larger than OPEN_MAX (20).
.TP 15
[EFAULT]
.I fds
is not in the caller's memory.
.SH NOTES
The timeout is rounded up to the clock tick (1/60 s) and is never shorter than
asked.
A process can wait on at most 4 different character devices at a time without
cost; with more, it looks again at every clock tick.
.PP
Until the drivers answer DEV_SELECT (planned for the TTY and the Flipper
devices), terminals and
.B /dev/buttons
count as always ready.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR read (2),
.BR write (2),
.BR pipe (2),
.BR alarm (2).
